let __v_3006 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"cheese\",\"Yes, usually: ageing eats almost all of the lactose, so a hard cheese such as cheddar or parmesan is the dairy food most people with a lactose intolerance can eat without trouble. Soft cheese - brie, cottage cheese, ricotta, mozzarella - has more of it left, so try a small amount with a meal first.\"],[\"butter\",\"Yes: butter is mostly fat with only a trace of milk sugar left in it, so a normal spread is fine for most people with a lactose intolerance. Cream and ghee are the same idea; milk, yoghurt and ice cream are the ones with real lactose in them.\"],[\"cream\",\"Yes, in a small amount: cream has far less lactose than milk, so a spoonful in a sauce is usually fine. Milk, soft cheese, yoghurt and ice cream are where most of the lactose is.\"],[\"milk\",\"Not much of it, and not on its own: milk is where most of the lactose is, so a small glass with a meal is the test, and lactose-free milk is ordinary milk with the sugar already split - it does the same job in tea, on cereal and in cooking.\"],[\"yoghurt\",\"Often yes: live yoghurt is easier than milk because the cultures do part of the digesting, so a small pot with a meal is usually fine. A big tub on its own, or ice cream, is where people notice it.\"],[\"yogurt\",\"Often yes: live yogurt is easier than milk because the cultures do part of the digesting, so a small pot with a meal is usually fine. A big tub on its own, or ice cream, is where people notice it.\"],[\"ice cream\",\"Not much of it: ice cream is milk and cream with sugar on top, so it is one of the higher ones. A couple of spoons after a meal is a different thing from a whole tub, and lactase tablets taken with the first mouthful cover it.\"]]}")?;
frame.store("_LACTOSE_SAYS", __v_3006.clone())?;
