let __v_5488 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"tea\",\"teas\"]},\"tea\",\"Black, green, white and oolong are the same leaf, picked and processed differently: black is fully oxidised and malty, green is steamed or pan-fired and grassy, white is the young leaf dried, and oolong sits between green and black. Everything else - peppermint, chamomile, rooibos, fruit - is a herbal infusion rather than tea, so it brews and keeps differently.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"pasta\",\"pastas\"]},\"pasta\",\"Long pasta - spaghetti, linguine, tagliatelle - is for thin sauces that coat it. Short shapes - penne, fusilli, rigatoni - hold chunkier sauces, and the ridges and tubes are there to catch them. Filled pasta - ravioli, tortellini - is a meal with its sauce inside. Dried is durum wheat and water; fresh is egg and soft wheat, cooks in two or three minutes, and is richer. Wholewheat is the same shapes with the bran left in, and takes a minute longer.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"oil\",\"oils\"]},\"oil\",\"Olive oil splits in two: extra virgin is the cold-pressed first press, peppery and best raw or for low heat, and ordinary olive oil is refined, plainer and fine for frying. Rapeseed is the mild all-rounder and the cheapest British one. Sunflower and vegetable oil are neutral and take high heat, which is what chips and stir-fries want. Sesame is for flavour, a teaspoon at the end, and coconut oil tastes of coconut. Groundnut oil is the one restaurants fry in, because it is neutral and stable when hot.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"honey\",\"honeys\"]},\"honey\",\"It is named for the flower the bees worked: clover and acacia are light and mild, heather and chestnut are dark and strong, and orange blossom is perfumed. Runny or set is the same honey - set has simply crystallised, which a warm jug of water undoes. Raw or unfiltered means it has not been heated, so it is cloudier and tastes more of the flowers. Never give any honey to a baby under one, because of the botulism risk.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"milk\",\"milks\"]},\"milk\",\"Cow's milk comes by fat: whole at about 3.5%, semi-skimmed at 1.7% and skimmed at almost nothing, and the fat is what carries the taste in coffee and sauces. Fresh and UHT are the same milk, one pasteurised hotter so it keeps for months unopened. Goat's and sheep's are the other animals, sharper and richer. Plant milks - oat, soya, almond, coconut - are a different drink, and the barista ones are the ones that do not split in hot coffee.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bean\",\"beans\"]},\"bean\",\"Dried or tinned is the first split: dried needs soaking overnight and a hard ten-minute boil, tinned is cooked and ready. After that it is the bean: kidney is the chilli one, cannellini and haricot are creamy and white (baked beans are haricot in tomato), black beans are earthy for Mexican food, and butter beans are the big soft ones. Chickpeas, lentils and split peas are the same family - pulses - and they cook without soaking.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lettuce\",\"lettuces\"]},\"lettuce\",\"Crisphead - iceberg - is the crunchy one that keeps for a week and is mostly water. Cos, or romaine, is the upright one with a spine, and it is the one for Caesar. Butterhead - round and little gem - is soft and sweet, and wilts first. Loose-leaf - oakleaf, lollo rosso, frisée - is the curly one to tear into a bowl. Rocket and spinach are leaves sold beside it, not lettuce, and they are peppery or earthy rather than crisp.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"wine\",\"wines\"]},\"wine\",\"Red, white and rosé are about the skin, not the grape: red is fermented on it, white off it, and rosé briefly on it. Sparkling is a second fermentation for the bubbles, and fortified - sherry, port, madeira - has spirit added, which is why it keeps for weeks after opening. The other axis is the grape and where it grew, which is what makes a Chablis lean and a Chardonnay from a hot place buttery, and a wine dry or sweet.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"coffee\",\"coffees\"]},\"coffee\",\"Two beans: arabica is the fruity, slightly acidic one in most good coffee, and robusta is stronger, more bitter and has about twice the caffeine, which is why instant and cheap espresso blends use it. Roast runs from light, which keeps the fruit and the acidity, to dark, which tastes of the roast itself. Then it is ground to the brew: fine for espresso, medium for a filter machine, coarse for a cafetière. Instant is brewed and dried, a different drink, not a weaker one.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"yoghurt\",\"yogurt\",\"yoghurts\",\"yogurts\"]},\"yoghurt\",\"Natural is milk and cultures and nothing else, and it is what to cook with. Greek is the same yoghurt strained of its whey, so it is thicker and higher in protein; Greek-style is thickened with milk powder and is cheaper and sweeter. Set is cultured in the pot and stays firm, stirred is smooth. Skyr is strained further still, so it is very thick and very high in protein. Live yoghurt has the cultures still alive, which is the kind that helps an unsettled stomach.\"]}]}")?;
